== Source-family audit ==

* '''Annual Review of Psychology''' — working memory, grounded/embodied cognition, executive/cognitive-process reviews.
* '''Foundational cognitive psychology''' — Baddeley & Hitch; Atkinson/Shiffrin; Tulving; Posner; Kahneman; Flavell; Nelson & Narens.
* '''Judgment and decision research''' — Tversky & Kahneman; Gigerenzer; Nickerson; framing/anchoring literature.
* '''Cognitive load theory''' — Sweller and later educational-psychology reviews.
* '''Social cognition''' — Epley/Waytz/Cacioppo on anthropomorphism; developmental and comparative theory-of-mind literature.
* '''Distributed cognition''' — Hutchins; Hollan/Hutchins/Kirsh.
* '''Embodied/grounded cognition''' — Barsalou and 4E-cognition literature.
* '''Thought suppression''' — Wegner and Wenzlaff historical work plus contemporary review evidence evaluating effectiveness/rebound.
* '''Statistics/epidemiology/causal inference''' — selection bias.
* Wikipedia/Scholarpedia are orientation/discovery surfaces only where stronger sources exist.

== Title and scope risks ==

* [[Learning]] must remain biological/cognitive learning, not absorb [[Machine learning]].
* [[Memory]] and [[Working memory]] must remain separate.
* [[Reasoning]] remains broad psychological reasoning; formal deductive/inductive/abductive modes remain Wave 5 descendants.
* [[Attention]] must not inherit Transformer-specific meaning.
* [[Salience]] must remain broad enough to cover perceptual and motivational prominence without becoming a synonym for attention.
* [[Selection bias]] remains in the wave only as a high-value adjacent contrast and will use Science/Mathematics rather than Cognitive science as its Core Domain.
* "Pink elephant bias" is not promoted to a canonical concept. If a public alias is later useful, it should redirect to [[Thought suppression]] or an explanatory section rather than create a new knowledge identity.

